      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/scc-indigenous-sentencing-options-9.7020398?cmp=rss</guid>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/3a028bfa-fb5d-4fcf-8e26-ef446fbea45b,1766101675718/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C29%2C1079%2C606%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A woman wearing sunglasses and a man in a hooded jacket pose for a selfie outside. ' width='620' height='349' title='Brittany Sack, left, and her former partner Harry Cope are seen in Truro, N.S., in 2011. '/><p>The Mi'kmaw woman at the centre of an aggravated assault case now before the Supreme Court of Canada spoke about what she went through for the first time ever in an interview with CBC News. She says it's up to courts to find "balance" between sentencing Indigenous offenders and protecting Indigenous women who are victims.</p>]]></description>
